# Reverse lookup (crystod-group --parent G --child H); testsuite section 19
# Counterpart of the reverse search of ISOSUBGROUP (ISOTROPY Software Suite,
# https://iso.byu.edu): every stratum of every irrep at the special k points of
# G whose isotropy subgroup has type H, with the cell size, the index and the
# conventional basis and origin of the subgroup in the parent setting.
# The first run for a parent builds the table of all strata (one progress line
# per k point on stderr) and caches it as
# $CRYSTOD_CACHE_DIR/isotropy/<number>_<version>.json.gz
# (default ~/.cache/crystod/isotropy/...); later runs read the file.
# Several rows of one type are different embeddings of H in G (orientation or
# origin of the subgroup cell). --size, --index and --kpoint filter the rows;
# --no-cache rebuilds the table (a table built from other sources is rebuilt
# too). In a parent with improper operations, a row of the enantiomorphic
# partner type counts as a match (with a note); in a Sohncke parent such as
# P4_222 it does not.
# --coupled also searches the pairs of irreps (same k point first, then
# different k points) and lists the coupled strata of type H that need both
# irreps: the Pnma tilt system of the perovskites (size 4) is R4+ (0,a,a) +
# M3+ (d;0;0), the hybrid improper ferroelectric Cmc2_1 of I4/mmm is X2+ (0;a)
# + X3- (0;c). --secondary adds the secondary order parameters of every row.
# The progress lines of the coupled search (stderr) appear once it has run for
# a few seconds.

$ crystod-group --parent Pm-3m --child I4/mcm
